WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.383 --> 00:00:06.363
What happens when a neuroblast fails to sort its cargo?

2
00:00:07.243 --> 00:00:09.323
And why can one small error grow so large?

3
00:00:10.317 --> 00:00:15.977
In 2005, Emmanuel Caussinus and Cayetano Gonzalez ran a transplant test.

4
00:00:16.950 --> 00:00:22.670
They put larval brain tissue from pins, miranda, numb or prospero mutants into adult

5
00:00:22.670 --> 00:00:23.670
flies.

6
00:00:24.100 --> 00:00:27.540
The tissue grew to more than a hundred times its starting size.

7
00:00:28.440 --> 00:00:30.780
It invaded other tissues and killed its hosts.

8
00:00:33.783 --> 00:00:39.323
In 2006, Jörg Betschinger, Karl Mechtler and Jürgen Knoblich studied Brat and

9
00:00:39.323 --> 00:00:40.323
Prospero.

10
00:00:40.883 --> 00:00:45.883
In brat or prospero mutants, both daughters grew like neuroblasts and formed brain

11
00:00:45.883 --> 00:00:46.883
tumours.

12
00:00:47.333 --> 00:00:49.833
Later work showed the two guard different cells.

13
00:00:50.813 --> 00:00:55.493
Brat tumours arise mainly in type II lineages, from intermediate progenitors that

14
00:00:55.493 --> 00:00:56.493
fail to mature.

15
00:00:57.267 --> 00:01:00.427
Prospero tumours arise from ganglion mother cells.

16
00:01:01.467 --> 00:01:05.487
But how can a small leak at every division grow more than a hundredfold?

17
00:01:06.550 --> 00:01:09.410
Take one neuroblast that divides once per round.

18
00:01:10.210 --> 00:01:14.010
With perfect sorting, each small daughter becomes a ganglion mother cell.

19
00:01:14.983 --> 00:01:19.543
With leaky sorting, each small daughter has a chance, f, of staying a neuroblast.

20
00:01:20.503 --> 00:01:22.543
Every neuroblast divides in the next round.

21
00:01:23.533 --> 00:01:27.513
Run 50 rounds. How many neuroblasts with perfect sorting?

22
00:01:28.453 --> 00:01:31.413
How many if one small daughter in ten stays a neuroblast?

23
00:01:37.483 --> 00:01:40.263
With perfect sorting, there is still one neuroblast.

24
00:01:41.183 --> 00:01:43.343
Its output just adds up, round by round.

25
00:01:44.367 --> 00:01:48.187
With the leak, the neuroblasts multiply every round, on average.

26
00:01:49.127 --> 00:01:50.687
The count compounds.

27
00:01:51.117 --> 00:01:56.277
With one in ten leaking, the average passes a hundred neuroblasts after 50 rounds.

28
00:01:59.800 --> 00:02:02.460
These round counts are illustrative, not measured.

29
00:02:03.300 --> 00:02:05.640
The model counts neuroblasts, not tissue size.

30
00:02:06.500 --> 00:02:08.440
It is not a fit to the transplant data.

31
00:02:09.433 --> 00:02:14.453
It shows only that a small leak at each division, compounded, can multiply the

32
00:02:14.453 --> 00:02:16.353
dividing cells more than a hundredfold.

33
00:02:17.467 --> 00:02:19.607
In mammals, the picture is less clear.

34
00:02:20.587 --> 00:02:22.847
Mice have Numb and a second relative, Numblike.

35
00:02:23.800 --> 00:02:27.600
Removing both early in the nervous system used up progenitors.

36
00:02:28.583 --> 00:02:32.863
Removing both in the dorsal forebrain, studied across the later peak of

37
00:02:32.863 --> 00:02:35.883
neurogenesis, made progenitors overproliferate.

38
00:02:36.817 --> 00:02:39.977
The two studies differ in where and when Numb was removed.

39
00:02:40.837 --> 00:02:42.677
Which difference matters is not settled.

40
00:02:43.717 --> 00:02:46.057
Numb also has other jobs in these cells.

41
00:02:46.577 --> 00:02:49.797
So a mouse phenotype is not automatically a Notch veto.

42
00:02:50.867 --> 00:02:52.507
One question points onward.

43
00:02:53.587 --> 00:02:54.847
Numb makes two sisters different.

44
00:02:55.667 --> 00:02:58.307
It does not say which of many neuron types they become.

45
00:02:59.300 --> 00:03:03.180
That depends on when a cell was born, the subject of the next lesson.

46
00:03:04.183 --> 00:03:07.583
A rigged division leaks self-renewal into the wrong daughter.

47
00:03:08.563 --> 00:03:11.543
Compounded, a small leak can multiply the dividing cells.
